1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,630
Before anything else, let's play a game.

2
00:00:02,760 --> 00:00:03,790
Three questions.

3
00:00:04,080 --> 00:00:05,730
You don't have to answer out loud.

4
00:00:05,820 --> 00:00:09,610
Just do one thing:
notice how sure you feel.

5
00:00:10,020 --> 00:00:11,630
Let's go with the first one.

6
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:13,750
What is the capital of France?

7
00:00:16,110 --> 00:00:18,340
You didn't even search, did you.

8
00:00:18,570 --> 00:00:21,360
The answer was there before
the question had finished.

9
00:00:21,730 --> 00:00:23,140
Notice that feeling.

10
00:00:23,570 --> 00:00:25,800
Certainty. It has a texture.

11
00:00:26,150 --> 00:00:27,380
Second question.

12
00:00:27,630 --> 00:00:30,400
Who painted The Garden
of Earthly Delights?

13
00:00:32,850 --> 00:00:36,100
Maybe you have it. Maybe it's
on the tip of your tongue.

14
00:00:36,350 --> 00:00:38,140
And here is the strange part:

15
00:00:38,350 --> 00:00:42,160
even if you cannot say the name,
you know something about it.

16
00:00:42,270 --> 00:00:44,960
You know whether you would
recognise it if I said it.

17
00:00:45,130 --> 00:00:48,740
That feeling has a name,
a paddle, and a neuron.

18
00:00:48,850 --> 00:00:50,420
We will get to all three.

19
00:00:50,570 --> 00:00:51,660
Final question.

20
00:00:51,970 --> 00:00:56,830
What is the capital of the
Martian colony Alpha-three?

21
00:00:57,470 --> 00:01:00,040
Nothing. And you know it's nothing.

22
00:01:00,130 --> 00:01:03,900
Not "I forgot",
but "there is nothing to find".

23
00:01:04,150 --> 00:01:07,860
That feeling is different
again. Clean. Immediate.

24
00:01:08,510 --> 00:01:09,600
Three questions.

25
00:01:09,810 --> 00:01:14,520
Three different feelings, not about the
world, but about your own knowledge.

26
00:01:14,750 --> 00:01:17,240
Nobody taught you that. Nobody had to.

27
00:01:17,670 --> 00:01:21,280
Now watch. The same three
questions, to a machine.

28
00:01:21,510 --> 00:01:26,600
A program built, first of all,
to do one thing: predict the next word.

29
00:01:26,970 --> 00:01:29,680
Sure. Sure again. And then:

30
00:01:29,830 --> 00:01:35,512
"I'm not aware of any information about
a Martian colony called Alpha-three."

31
00:01:35,930 --> 00:01:37,780
Stay with that for a second.

32
00:01:38,050 --> 00:01:40,370
Nobody wrote that answer into it.

33
00:01:40,430 --> 00:01:43,050
There is no list,
anywhere in this program,

34
00:01:43,110 --> 00:01:46,130
of the things it knows and
the things it doesn't.

35
00:01:46,190 --> 00:01:49,880
And yet the doubt is there,
in you, and in it.

36
00:01:50,250 --> 00:01:52,360
So where does it come from?

37
00:01:52,750 --> 00:01:56,520
That is the question of this film.
And I'll make you a promise.

38
00:01:56,600 --> 00:01:59,240
By the end, we will open that machine,

39
00:01:59,390 --> 00:02:04,300
read its doubt before it has said a single
word, and catch it saying the opposite.

40
00:02:05,090 --> 00:02:08,260
But to get there,
we have to start much earlier.

41
00:02:08,470 --> 00:02:10,310
Two and a half thousand years earlier,

42
00:02:10,370 --> 00:02:14,200
with a man who claimed to
know exactly one thing.

43
00:02:19,770 --> 00:02:25,260
Carved at the entrance of the temple
of Delphi, two words: know thyself.

44
00:02:25,710 --> 00:02:27,340
It sounds like advice.

45
00:02:27,630 --> 00:02:29,020
Good advice, even.

46
00:02:29,330 --> 00:02:31,790
For one man in Athens,
it became a problem,

47
00:02:31,850 --> 00:02:34,860
because he took it literally,
and went to check.

48
00:02:35,290 --> 00:02:39,560
Let's go to Athens, in the fifth
century BC, and check with him.

49
00:02:40,350 --> 00:02:45,300
The oracle of Delphi had declared that no
one in Athens was wiser than Socrates.

50
00:02:45,770 --> 00:02:49,840
Socrates did not believe it.
So he did the obvious thing.

51
00:02:50,090 --> 00:02:52,710
He went to find the people who
had a reputation for wisdom,

52
00:02:52,770 --> 00:02:54,580
and he asked them questions.

53
00:02:55,210 --> 00:02:57,960
A politician. Sure of everything.

54
00:02:58,230 --> 00:03:01,860
Three questions from Socrates,
and the certainty came apart.

55
00:03:02,210 --> 00:03:03,960
A poet. The same.

56
00:03:04,090 --> 00:03:06,730
A craftsman, who really
did know his craft,

57
00:03:06,790 --> 00:03:10,180
and for that very reason believed
he knew everything else.

58
00:03:10,410 --> 00:03:12,000
Each of them was sure.

59
00:03:12,350 --> 00:03:15,000
None of them was right
about how much he knew.

60
00:03:15,550 --> 00:03:17,740
And Socrates drew his conclusion.

61
00:03:18,070 --> 00:03:20,320
Not "I know more than they do."

62
00:03:20,450 --> 00:03:25,380
Something stranger: I am wiser
than this man by one small thing.

63
00:03:25,710 --> 00:03:29,040
What I do not know,
I do not think I know either.

64
00:03:30,390 --> 00:03:32,300
Look at what he is claiming.

65
00:03:32,570 --> 00:03:33,580
Not knowledge.

66
00:03:33,770 --> 00:03:36,940
An accurate picture of the
limits of his knowledge.

67
00:03:37,370 --> 00:03:41,900
That is one of the first written claims
of what we now call metacognition:

68
00:03:42,190 --> 00:03:44,080
knowing about your own knowing.

69
00:03:44,170 --> 00:03:46,680
And it is the whole subject of this film.

70
00:03:47,090 --> 00:03:50,200
So let us write it down once, and keep it.

71
00:03:50,310 --> 00:03:56,020
Two lines. The first: what you know.
The second: what you think you know.

72
00:03:56,130 --> 00:03:58,300
The second line is about the first.

73
00:03:58,670 --> 00:04:01,540
When the two lines agree,
you are Socrates.

74
00:04:02,010 --> 00:04:05,740
When the second claims more than
the first, you are the politician.

75
00:04:05,990 --> 00:04:10,220
Every single thing in this film
is a story about those two lines:

76
00:04:10,450 --> 00:04:13,100
the dolphin, the neuron, the machine.

77
00:04:13,570 --> 00:04:18,280
For two thousand years, that second
line stayed a philosopher's curiosity.

78
00:04:18,590 --> 00:04:22,460
Then Descartes picked it up and
pushed it as far as it would go.

79
00:04:22,950 --> 00:04:25,160
He decided to doubt everything.

80
00:04:25,490 --> 00:04:29,000
The world: gone. His own body: gone.

81
00:04:29,270 --> 00:04:31,020
His memories: gone.

82
00:04:31,270 --> 00:04:34,480
Everything that could possibly
be a dream or a trick.

83
00:04:34,850 --> 00:04:35,850
What survived?

84
00:04:37,310 --> 00:04:38,310
One thing.

85
00:04:38,530 --> 00:04:39,760
The doubting itself.

86
00:04:40,230 --> 00:04:42,480
I cannot doubt that I am doubting.

87
00:04:42,670 --> 00:04:47,020
The last certainty standing was
a certainty about his own mind.

88
00:04:47,230 --> 00:04:48,740
Notice what that does.

89
00:04:48,822 --> 00:04:51,820
Self-knowledge is no longer a curiosity.

90
00:04:52,170 --> 00:04:55,090
It has become the foundation
everything else is built on.

91
00:04:55,150 --> 00:04:58,910
The philosophers who followed
gave that faculty a name,

92
00:04:58,970 --> 00:05:02,460
reflection, and,
more importantly, a picture.

93
00:05:02,870 --> 00:05:05,780
The mind knows itself
by looking at itself.

94
00:05:06,070 --> 00:05:07,900
Introspection is a window.

95
00:05:08,150 --> 00:05:11,800
You turn your attention inward,
and you see the machinery turn.

96
00:05:12,370 --> 00:05:15,060
For two centuries, that was the picture.

97
00:05:15,470 --> 00:05:16,900
It was about to break.

98
00:05:17,670 --> 00:05:22,280
In eighteen thirty, Auguste Comte
broke the window with one observation.

99
00:05:22,710 --> 00:05:26,820
Try it yourself, right now.
Try to watch yourself think.

100
00:05:27,830 --> 00:05:30,680
The moment you look, the thinking stops.

101
00:05:30,970 --> 00:05:31,810
It has to,

102
00:05:31,870 --> 00:05:36,340
because the thing that is looking and the
thing that is thinking are the same thing.

103
00:05:36,570 --> 00:05:40,920
The thinking individual, Comte wrote,
cannot split himself in two,

104
00:05:41,090 --> 00:05:44,460
one part reasoning, while the
other part watches it reason.

105
00:05:44,830 --> 00:05:48,580
Introspection, he concluded,
is impossible by construction.

106
00:05:49,150 --> 00:05:52,100
Then, obviously, philosophers replied.

107
00:05:52,470 --> 00:05:57,100
For Mill: we do observe our thoughts,
a moment later, through memory.

108
00:05:57,410 --> 00:06:02,050
For Brentano: we do not observe them,
we perceive them, on the side,

109
00:06:02,110 --> 00:06:04,060
while attending to something else.

110
00:06:04,530 --> 00:06:07,480
Whoever was right, something had shifted.

111
00:06:07,610 --> 00:06:10,900
The question was no longer
"how clear is the window?"

112
00:06:11,110 --> 00:06:15,450
The question had become: is self-knowledge
a view from the inside at all?

113
00:06:15,510 --> 00:06:19,150
Or is it a signal: something
the mind produces about itself,

114
00:06:19,210 --> 00:06:21,780
which may be accurate, or may not?

115
00:06:21,970 --> 00:06:23,980
Keep that question in your pocket.

116
00:06:24,130 --> 00:06:26,640
It will decide everything about machines.

117
00:06:27,290 --> 00:06:30,980
The window was tried anyway,
properly, with laboratories.

118
00:06:31,530 --> 00:06:35,700
From eighteen seventy-nine,
in Wilhelm Wundt's laboratory in Leipzig,

119
00:06:35,910 --> 00:06:38,800
trained observers reported
on their own sensations.

120
00:06:39,210 --> 00:06:42,980
The same light. Reddish. Orange-ish. Warm.

121
00:06:43,250 --> 00:06:45,210
More than thirty years of that.

122
00:06:45,270 --> 00:06:50,020
The laboratories could not agree on what
they saw, and they had no way to settle it.

123
00:06:50,190 --> 00:06:53,960
In nineteen thirteen,
John Watson closed the door.

124
00:06:54,190 --> 00:06:56,280
Of the new psychology, he wrote:

125
00:06:56,430 --> 00:06:59,900
introspection forms no
essential part of its methods.

126
00:07:00,430 --> 00:07:05,320
For half a century, psychology would
study behaviour only, from the outside.

127
00:07:06,130 --> 00:07:07,640
The window was shut.

128
00:07:07,910 --> 00:07:11,840
And that, oddly, is what made
the next discovery possible.

129
00:07:12,110 --> 00:07:16,450
Because if you cannot look inside,
you have to measure from outside.

130
00:07:16,510 --> 00:07:19,680
And when psychologists
finally did that carefully,

131
00:07:19,790 --> 00:07:22,060
they found something nobody expected.

132
00:07:22,530 --> 00:07:24,310
In nineteen seventy-seven,

133
00:07:24,370 --> 00:07:27,930
Richard Nisbett and Timothy Wilson
published a paper with a title

134
00:07:27,990 --> 00:07:32,320
that is almost a warning:
Telling More Than We Can Know.

135
00:07:32,750 --> 00:07:34,440
Here is one of their experiments.

136
00:07:34,830 --> 00:07:38,710
In a store, they laid out four pairs
of nylon stockings on a table,

137
00:07:38,770 --> 00:07:41,560
and asked people to pick the best one.

138
00:07:42,390 --> 00:07:44,760
The four pairs were identical.

139
00:07:45,310 --> 00:07:49,570
People chose the pair on the right far
more often than the pair on the left:

140
00:07:49,630 --> 00:07:53,500
by almost four to one,
in the authors' own words.

141
00:07:53,930 --> 00:07:55,360
Pure position effect.

142
00:07:55,650 --> 00:07:58,040
The stockings had nothing to do with it.

143
00:07:58,250 --> 00:08:00,160
Then came the interesting part.

144
00:08:00,510 --> 00:08:03,720
They asked each person why
they had chosen that pair.

145
00:08:04,590 --> 00:08:06,060
People gave reasons.

146
00:08:06,330 --> 00:08:11,520
The knit. The sheerness. The quality.
They were confident, and specific.

147
00:08:11,690 --> 00:08:14,430
And when the experimenters asked
whether the position on the

148
00:08:14,490 --> 00:08:18,400
table could have influenced them,
almost everyone said no.

149
00:08:18,510 --> 00:08:21,820
And they looked at the experimenter
as if he were slightly mad.

150
00:08:23,350 --> 00:08:25,920
They were not lying. That is the point.

151
00:08:26,150 --> 00:08:30,040
They simply had no access to the
process that had made the choice.

152
00:08:30,290 --> 00:08:35,420
So they did what minds do: they told
a plausible story, and believed it.

153
00:08:35,550 --> 00:08:37,940
You would have done the same. So would I.

154
00:08:38,750 --> 00:08:43,310
This is the state of the art,
a hundred and fifty years after Comte.

155
00:08:43,370 --> 00:08:45,520
Introspection is not a window.

156
00:08:45,810 --> 00:08:46,920
It is a narrator.

157
00:08:47,230 --> 00:08:49,410
A narrator who speaks with confidence,

158
00:08:49,470 --> 00:08:52,780
and who is often wrong about
what is happening behind him.

159
00:08:53,030 --> 00:08:55,960
So here is the rule this
whole film will follow.

160
00:08:56,250 --> 00:08:58,780
The verbal report is not the measurement.

161
00:08:59,090 --> 00:09:01,100
Person, animal or machine:

162
00:09:01,290 --> 00:09:05,530
if we want to know whether it knows
what it knows, we cannot just ask it.

163
00:09:05,590 --> 00:09:09,180
We have to measure it from
the outside, by its effects.

164
00:09:09,570 --> 00:09:13,840
Which raises a practical question:
measure what, exactly?

165
00:09:14,130 --> 00:09:16,000
What would we even be looking for?

166
00:09:16,510 --> 00:09:18,190
In the nineteen-seventies,

167
00:09:18,250 --> 00:09:22,880
the psychologist John Flavell gave
the field its name: metacognition.

168
00:09:23,090 --> 00:09:25,230
And in nineteen ninety, Thomas Nelson

169
00:09:25,290 --> 00:09:29,640
and Louis Narens drew the diagram we
will use for the rest of this film.

170
00:09:29,890 --> 00:09:33,240
It is our two lines,
with two arrows added.

171
00:09:33,850 --> 00:09:34,850
Two levels.

172
00:09:35,090 --> 00:09:39,980
At the bottom, the object level:
perceiving, remembering, answering.

173
00:09:40,090 --> 00:09:41,420
That's the first line.

174
00:09:41,650 --> 00:09:46,340
At the top, the meta level: a model
of what the object level is doing.

175
00:09:46,490 --> 00:09:47,500
That's the second.

176
00:09:48,190 --> 00:09:50,100
And two flows between them.

177
00:09:50,350 --> 00:09:53,860
Going up, monitoring:
how is it going down there?

178
00:09:54,050 --> 00:09:58,860
Going down, control: keep going,
stop, try something else.

179
00:09:59,270 --> 00:10:00,740
You have felt both.

180
00:10:01,050 --> 00:10:04,500
You are reading a page and realise
you have taken in nothing.

181
00:10:04,790 --> 00:10:07,670
That noticing: that is monitoring.

182
00:10:08,040 --> 00:10:09,830
You go back to the top of the page.

183
00:10:10,100 --> 00:10:11,570
That is control.

184
00:10:12,080 --> 00:10:17,190
Nelson and Narens gave their own examples,
from a student revising for an exam.

185
00:10:17,620 --> 00:10:20,310
This one looks hard: study it longer.

186
00:10:20,720 --> 00:10:23,630
I've got this one now: stop studying it.

187
00:10:23,840 --> 00:10:26,710
I know it's in there somewhere:
keep searching.

188
00:10:27,020 --> 00:10:31,090
Each time, a judgment goes up,
and a decision comes down.

189
00:10:31,580 --> 00:10:35,690
Keep this diagram in mind.
It has a remarkable property.

190
00:10:36,020 --> 00:10:39,810
It says nothing about language.
Nothing about introspection.

191
00:10:39,940 --> 00:10:41,710
Nothing about consciousness.

192
00:10:42,240 --> 00:10:45,640
Which means we are free to go
looking for it in a dolphin.

193
00:10:45,700 --> 00:10:48,170
In a monkey. In a rat's neurons.

194
00:10:48,660 --> 00:10:51,450
And, eventually, in a machine.

195
00:10:57,160 --> 00:11:01,130
Let's start with the feeling you had
in the first minute of this film.

196
00:11:01,420 --> 00:11:05,170
Someone asks you a question.
You cannot find the answer.

197
00:11:05,400 --> 00:11:08,890
And yet you are sure that you
would recognise it if you saw it.

198
00:11:09,460 --> 00:11:11,070
Is that feeling real?

199
00:11:11,160 --> 00:11:15,630
Or is it just a story: another narrator,
telling more than it knows?

200
00:11:16,380 --> 00:11:21,410
In nineteen sixty-five, the psychologist
Joseph Hart decided to test it.

201
00:11:21,660 --> 00:11:24,120
He asked people questions
they could not answer,

202
00:11:24,180 --> 00:11:28,970
and asked them to rate how confident they
were that they would recognise the answer.

203
00:11:29,220 --> 00:11:31,410
Then he showed them a list of options.

204
00:11:32,000 --> 00:11:34,370
You have just been through his experiment.

205
00:11:34,600 --> 00:11:36,620
The painter was Hieronymus Bosch.

206
00:11:36,680 --> 00:11:40,730
If you felt you would recognise
the name, you probably just did.

207
00:11:41,340 --> 00:11:42,710
The feeling was right.

208
00:11:43,040 --> 00:11:45,060
When people said they would
recognise the answer,

209
00:11:45,120 --> 00:11:49,070
they recognised it far more often
than when they said they would not.

210
00:11:49,200 --> 00:11:52,050
Not perfectly. But well above chance.

211
00:11:52,420 --> 00:11:55,210
The narrator, for once, knew something.

212
00:11:55,700 --> 00:11:59,790
A year later, Brown and McNeill
went after the extreme case:

213
00:12:00,060 --> 00:12:02,210
the word on the tip of your tongue.

214
00:12:02,460 --> 00:12:05,800
People who could not produce a word
could often say its first letter,

215
00:12:05,860 --> 00:12:09,610
its number of syllables,
a word that sounded like it.

216
00:12:09,680 --> 00:12:13,890
The memory was there, partly.
And the mind knew it was there.

217
00:12:14,160 --> 00:12:17,950
But here is the subtle part,
and it took decades to see.

218
00:12:18,260 --> 00:12:20,720
Later work, by Asher Koriat among others,

219
00:12:20,780 --> 00:12:24,660
showed that these feelings are
not a direct reading of memory.

220
00:12:24,720 --> 00:12:26,230
They are inferences.

221
00:12:26,560 --> 00:12:30,280
The mind uses cues:
how familiar the question feels,

222
00:12:30,340 --> 00:12:34,360
how quickly fragments come back.
From those, it builds an estimate.

223
00:12:34,420 --> 00:12:37,580
And when a question finds
nothing at all in memory,

224
00:12:37,640 --> 00:12:42,230
like the Martian colony, the verdict
comes back at once: nothing to find.

225
00:12:43,000 --> 00:12:46,930
So already, in humans,
metacognition is a signal.

226
00:12:47,200 --> 00:12:49,730
Real. Useful. Imperfect.

227
00:12:49,980 --> 00:12:52,970
Constructed from evidence,
not read from a window.

228
00:12:53,080 --> 00:12:57,310
And a signal can be read from what
it does, not only from what it says.

229
00:12:57,400 --> 00:13:01,190
Which means we can go looking for
it somewhere words have never been.

230
00:13:01,480 --> 00:13:04,910
Can we find the same thing in
a creature that cannot talk?

231
00:13:05,060 --> 00:13:09,950
This is the question that turns metacognition
from philosophy into a science.

232
00:13:10,160 --> 00:13:12,810
And the first answer came from a dolphin.

233
00:13:13,560 --> 00:13:15,200
In nineteen ninety-five,

234
00:13:15,260 --> 00:13:19,710
David Smith and his colleagues worked
with a bottlenose dolphin named Natua.

235
00:13:19,900 --> 00:13:22,670
The task was simple. A tone was played.

236
00:13:22,800 --> 00:13:27,220
If it was high, a whistle at exactly
two thousand one hundred hertz,

237
00:13:27,280 --> 00:13:29,310
Natua had to press one paddle.

238
00:13:29,440 --> 00:13:34,100
If it was lower, anything from twelve
hundred up to just below the high tone,

239
00:13:34,160 --> 00:13:35,530
he had to press the other.

240
00:13:35,980 --> 00:13:38,280
Easy when the tone is clearly low.

241
00:13:38,340 --> 00:13:41,330
Very hard when it is just
below the threshold.

242
00:13:41,800 --> 00:13:46,430
So far, nothing metacognitive:
a dolphin discriminating sounds.

243
00:13:47,220 --> 00:13:50,450
Then they added a third paddle. An escape.

244
00:13:50,740 --> 00:13:54,610
Pressing it skipped the current
trial and moved on to an easy one.

245
00:13:54,860 --> 00:13:56,770
Natua learned to use it.

246
00:13:56,840 --> 00:14:01,520
And he used it in exactly one place:
on the trials near the threshold,

247
00:14:01,580 --> 00:14:05,330
where his own errors were concentrated.
Not on the easy ones.

248
00:14:05,620 --> 00:14:07,250
You do the same in an exam.

249
00:14:07,460 --> 00:14:11,110
You skip the question you are not
sure of, and come back to it later.

250
00:14:11,540 --> 00:14:15,350
And there was something else,
something you could see with your eyes.

251
00:14:15,800 --> 00:14:20,050
On those hard trials, before
pressing anything, he hesitated.

252
00:14:20,340 --> 00:14:22,590
He wavered between the two paddles.

253
00:14:22,900 --> 00:14:26,310
On the easy trials,
he swam straight to his answer.

254
00:14:26,950 --> 00:14:28,460
Look at the diagram again.

255
00:14:28,730 --> 00:14:33,800
A task, at the object level.
A signal about how that task is going.

256
00:14:33,950 --> 00:14:37,860
And a control decision based
on that signal: skip this one.

257
00:14:37,990 --> 00:14:41,960
Without a word of language,
we get the second line, in a dolphin.

258
00:14:43,210 --> 00:14:48,660
Six years later, Robert Hampton did
something even cleaner, with rhesus monkeys.

259
00:14:48,910 --> 00:14:53,080
They saw an image.
Then a delay. Then a choice.

260
00:14:53,410 --> 00:14:56,270
Take a memory test:
pick that image among several,

261
00:14:56,330 --> 00:14:59,060
for a reward they liked, a peanut.

262
00:14:59,230 --> 00:15:03,880
Or decline the test, for a less
attractive but guaranteed reward.

263
00:15:04,270 --> 00:15:05,270
Two results.

264
00:15:05,650 --> 00:15:09,490
First, the monkeys declined more
often as the delay got longer,

265
00:15:09,550 --> 00:15:11,400
as their memory faded.

266
00:15:11,690 --> 00:15:14,430
Second, on the tests they chose to take,

267
00:15:14,490 --> 00:15:17,760
they were more accurate than on
tests they were forced to take.

268
00:15:17,850 --> 00:15:19,520
They knew when they remembered.

269
00:15:20,050 --> 00:15:22,390
And notice the detail
that makes this clean:

270
00:15:22,450 --> 00:15:27,000
at the moment of choosing,
the image was no longer on the screen.

271
00:15:27,290 --> 00:15:29,940
The monkey could not be
reacting to the stimulus.

272
00:15:30,150 --> 00:15:32,160
There was nothing left to react to.

273
00:15:32,310 --> 00:15:34,860
It was reading the state
of its own memory.

274
00:15:35,290 --> 00:15:40,320
Later, Kornell, Son and Terrace taught
monkeys to bet on their own answers.

275
00:15:40,690 --> 00:15:44,700
The task: which of several
boxes holds the most dots?

276
00:15:45,110 --> 00:15:46,840
Then the boxes vanish.

277
00:15:47,090 --> 00:15:53,040
No feedback. And the monkey bets.
A high bet: win big, or lose big.

278
00:15:53,410 --> 00:15:56,160
A low bet: a small win, guaranteed.

279
00:15:56,770 --> 00:15:59,420
They bet high more often
when they were right:

280
00:15:59,630 --> 00:16:03,440
a wager on their own answer,
with nothing left on the screen.

281
00:16:04,930 --> 00:16:08,240
Now, sceptics objected,
and they were right to.

282
00:16:08,390 --> 00:16:11,680
Maybe the animal is just following
a rule about the stimulus:

283
00:16:11,930 --> 00:16:14,740
"when the tone is in this
range, press escape."

284
00:16:14,970 --> 00:16:18,680
A reflex, wired in,
with no second line behind it.

285
00:16:18,870 --> 00:16:21,800
The field spent twenty
years on that objection.

286
00:16:22,270 --> 00:16:25,780
For most researchers,
the answer came from transfer tests:

287
00:16:25,950 --> 00:16:30,660
the animals still used the escape correctly,
with new stimuli and new tasks.

288
00:16:30,830 --> 00:16:35,500
And from memory tasks like Hampton's,
where there is no stimulus left to follow.

289
00:16:36,090 --> 00:16:39,500
So here is where we stand
at the end of the animals.

290
00:16:39,730 --> 00:16:43,050
The meta level is there.
It monitors. It controls.

291
00:16:43,110 --> 00:16:45,500
And it does not need language.

292
00:16:45,770 --> 00:16:50,000
Which leaves the obvious question:
if it is real, where is it?

293
00:16:50,090 --> 00:16:51,440
It has to be somewhere.

294
00:16:51,890 --> 00:16:56,520
In two thousand eight, Adam Kepecs
and his colleagues found it in a rat.

295
00:16:57,230 --> 00:17:02,240
The rat sniffed a mixture of two odours,
and had to report which one was stronger.

296
00:17:02,610 --> 00:17:05,080
Easy when one odour clearly dominated.

297
00:17:05,310 --> 00:17:08,360
Hard when the mixture was
close to half and half.

298
00:17:08,590 --> 00:17:12,960
You will recognise the design:
it is the dolphin's task, in smell.

299
00:17:13,590 --> 00:17:15,020
Here is the clever part.

300
00:17:15,370 --> 00:17:19,220
After answering, the rat had to
wait at the port for its reward.

301
00:17:19,470 --> 00:17:22,120
And it could give up,
and start a new trial.

302
00:17:22,510 --> 00:17:26,200
It gave up more often on the trials
where it was likely to be wrong.

303
00:17:26,570 --> 00:17:31,000
In a follow-up, the same lab measured
how long the rats were willing to wait.

304
00:17:31,290 --> 00:17:34,760
On easy, correct trials,
they waited patiently.

305
00:17:35,190 --> 00:17:38,960
On hard trials, or wrong ones,
they gave up sooner.

306
00:17:39,530 --> 00:17:43,860
The waiting was a measure of confidence,
read directly from behaviour.

307
00:17:44,290 --> 00:17:47,120
No paddle, no bet. Just patience.

308
00:17:47,750 --> 00:17:49,540
And now the part that matters.

309
00:17:49,910 --> 00:17:53,760
The two thousand eight study looked
in the orbitofrontal cortex,

310
00:17:53,910 --> 00:17:55,860
a region just behind the eyes.

311
00:17:56,110 --> 00:17:58,920
It found neurons that
followed the same pattern:

312
00:17:59,090 --> 00:18:02,640
stronger on difficult trials,
stronger on errors.

313
00:18:02,910 --> 00:18:04,570
A neural signal of confidence.

314
00:18:04,630 --> 00:18:08,400
For the first time,
the second line had an address.

315
00:18:09,090 --> 00:18:10,096
One year later,

316
00:18:10,156 --> 00:18:15,280
Roozbeh Kiani and Michael Shadlen
went one step further, in a monkey.

317
00:18:15,750 --> 00:18:20,520
The monkey watched a cloud of moving dots
and had to say which way they moved:

318
00:18:20,670 --> 00:18:21,940
left or right.

319
00:18:22,310 --> 00:18:25,540
Some trials were easy,
most dots moving together.

320
00:18:25,870 --> 00:18:27,900
Some were nearly impossible.

321
00:18:28,270 --> 00:18:31,850
And on some trials, a third
target appeared: a sure option.

322
00:18:31,910 --> 00:18:34,400
A small reward, guaranteed.

323
00:18:35,170 --> 00:18:36,380
As with the dolphin,

324
00:18:36,510 --> 00:18:40,780
the monkey picked the sure option
precisely when the evidence was weak.

325
00:18:41,490 --> 00:18:42,980
Now the key result.

326
00:18:43,450 --> 00:18:47,880
In a region called LIP, toward
the top and back of the head,

327
00:18:47,970 --> 00:18:53,100
there are neurons whose activity ramps up
as evidence accumulates toward a decision.

328
00:18:53,410 --> 00:18:56,740
Think of it as a counter,
climbing toward a threshold.

329
00:18:57,090 --> 00:19:00,600
When the counter reaches the
threshold, the monkey decides.

330
00:19:01,050 --> 00:19:03,740
When the monkey chose the sure option,

331
00:19:03,910 --> 00:19:08,720
those same neurons had climbed more slowly
and stopped at an intermediate level.

332
00:19:08,950 --> 00:19:10,000
Think about that.

333
00:19:10,170 --> 00:19:14,080
The confidence was not computed by
a separate module somewhere else.

334
00:19:14,150 --> 00:19:17,690
It was readable in the very same
circuit that was making the decision,

335
00:19:17,750 --> 00:19:20,260
in how far the counter had climbed.

336
00:19:20,730 --> 00:19:22,080
Remember this counter.

337
00:19:22,310 --> 00:19:26,950
In the third act, someone will read
confidence from inside an artificial network.

338
00:19:27,010 --> 00:19:29,680
It will look surprisingly like this.

339
00:19:30,370 --> 00:19:32,560
And it is not only rats and monkeys.

340
00:19:32,930 --> 00:19:37,180
Your brain also knows, very quickly,
when you have just made a mistake.

341
00:19:37,370 --> 00:19:38,720
Faster than you do.

342
00:19:39,150 --> 00:19:42,400
Here is the kind of task
that shows it. Try it.

343
00:19:42,630 --> 00:19:45,080
A row of arrows flashes on a screen.

344
00:19:45,470 --> 00:19:48,780
Left, or right: which way
does the middle one point?

345
00:19:48,970 --> 00:19:50,440
Only the middle one.

346
00:19:50,530 --> 00:19:52,900
When the others agree with it, it is easy.

347
00:19:53,010 --> 00:19:55,650
When they point the other way,
they pull at you.

348
00:19:55,710 --> 00:19:57,080
And you have to be fast.

349
00:19:57,210 --> 00:20:00,300
So now and then, your hand
follows the wrong arrows.

350
00:20:00,430 --> 00:20:01,590
You knew the rule.

351
00:20:01,650 --> 00:20:04,080
Your hand was simply
quicker than you were.

352
00:20:04,510 --> 00:20:08,200
And nothing tells you.
No buzzer. No red cross.

353
00:20:08,490 --> 00:20:10,080
The next arrows just appear.

354
00:20:10,710 --> 00:20:12,250
In the early nineties,

355
00:20:12,310 --> 00:20:17,420
two groups put electrodes on people's scalps
and gave them fast tasks of this kind:

356
00:20:17,710 --> 00:20:21,340
Falkenstein in Germany,
Gehring in the United States.

357
00:20:21,530 --> 00:20:25,840
Then they lined up every recording on
the instant of the press, and compared.

358
00:20:26,070 --> 00:20:28,620
After a correct press: nothing much.

359
00:20:28,690 --> 00:20:33,190
After a wrong one: a sharp negative
wave over the front of the head.

360
00:20:33,250 --> 00:20:36,480
About fifty to a hundred
milliseconds after the press.

361
00:20:36,710 --> 00:20:37,960
Before any feedback.

362
00:20:38,070 --> 00:20:41,020
Sometimes for errors the
person never even noticed.

363
00:20:41,610 --> 00:20:44,300
They called it the
error-related negativity.

364
00:20:44,790 --> 00:20:48,560
It is an internal error detector,
fast and automatic.

365
00:20:48,970 --> 00:20:52,330
It most likely comes from the
anterior cingulate cortex,

366
00:20:52,390 --> 00:20:55,220
deep in the middle of the brain,
behind the forehead.

367
00:20:55,830 --> 00:20:58,220
And it connects to the
counter we just saw.

368
00:20:58,450 --> 00:21:03,320
Confidence and error detection are two
readings of the same accumulating evidence.

369
00:21:03,610 --> 00:21:06,900
If the counter was low when
you acted, you feel unsure.

370
00:21:07,230 --> 00:21:10,900
And if evidence keeps coming in
against your choice after you acted,

371
00:21:10,990 --> 00:21:12,480
the error signal fires.

372
00:21:12,750 --> 00:21:14,820
Same machinery, read twice.

373
00:21:15,410 --> 00:21:18,260
We now have behaviour,
and we have neurons.

374
00:21:18,710 --> 00:21:21,200
We need one more piece
before the machines.

375
00:21:21,570 --> 00:21:26,940
A piece of arithmetic: a way to measure
metacognition separately from performance.

376
00:21:27,290 --> 00:21:29,100
The two are easy to confuse.

377
00:21:29,430 --> 00:21:32,160
With machines, that confusion will matter.

378
00:21:32,990 --> 00:21:36,330
Two people can score the same
on a test, say eighty percent,

379
00:21:36,390 --> 00:21:39,620
and still differ
completely in one respect.

380
00:21:39,950 --> 00:21:42,500
One of them knows which
answers she got wrong.

381
00:21:42,710 --> 00:21:45,180
Her confidence drops exactly on those.

382
00:21:45,450 --> 00:21:49,480
The other is equally confident
on everything, right or wrong.

383
00:21:49,910 --> 00:21:53,890
Psychologists call these
type one and type two.

384
00:21:53,950 --> 00:21:56,820
Type one: can you tell the stimuli apart?

385
00:21:57,310 --> 00:22:01,700
Type two: can you tell your own correct
answers from your own mistakes?

386
00:22:02,130 --> 00:22:06,570
Same score on type one.
Very different on type two.

387
00:22:06,630 --> 00:22:08,090
In two thousand twelve,

388
00:22:08,150 --> 00:22:14,620
Brian Maniscalco and Hakwan Lau gave type
two a proper scale, called meta-d-prime.

389
00:22:14,810 --> 00:22:20,460
Put simply: of the information she used to
answer, how much reaches her confidence?

390
00:22:20,910 --> 00:22:26,310
So you can finally ask, cleanly:
given how good this person is at the task,

391
00:22:26,370 --> 00:22:29,240
how good is she at
knowing how good she is?

392
00:22:29,550 --> 00:22:33,640
And there is a second question,
which sounds like the same one and is not.

393
00:22:34,190 --> 00:22:36,810
Sensitivity asks whether
your confidence separates

394
00:22:36,870 --> 00:22:39,520
your right answers from your wrong ones.

395
00:22:39,770 --> 00:22:42,820
Calibration asks whether
the numbers are honest.

396
00:22:42,930 --> 00:22:47,540
When you say seventy percent,
are you right seventy percent of the time?

397
00:22:47,970 --> 00:22:53,960
Draw it. On one axis, what people say.
On the other, how often they are right.

398
00:22:54,310 --> 00:22:57,580
A perfectly calibrated
person sits on the diagonal.

399
00:22:57,850 --> 00:23:01,300
Most of us do not.
On general-knowledge questions,

400
00:23:01,490 --> 00:23:04,300
when people say they are
one hundred percent sure,

401
00:23:04,410 --> 00:23:07,080
they are right about
three times out of four.

402
00:23:07,310 --> 00:23:10,360
Overconfidence is the rule,
not the exception.

403
00:23:10,510 --> 00:23:14,100
Think of the last time you said
you were one hundred percent sure.

404
00:23:14,370 --> 00:23:15,370
Were you right?

405
00:23:15,850 --> 00:23:19,440
There is a famous exception,
though: weather forecasters.

406
00:23:19,710 --> 00:23:22,360
When they say seventy
percent chance of rain,

407
00:23:22,430 --> 00:23:24,960
it rains about seventy
percent of the time.

408
00:23:25,150 --> 00:23:26,070
Why them?

409
00:23:26,130 --> 00:23:29,230
Because they make the same
kind of judgement every day,

410
00:23:29,290 --> 00:23:32,260
and every day the sky
tells them the answer.

411
00:23:32,530 --> 00:23:34,620
Feedback builds calibration.

412
00:23:35,290 --> 00:23:39,940
Sensitivity, and calibration:
the machines will be judged on both.

413
00:23:40,250 --> 00:23:45,280
And sensitivity, knowing when you are
right, has an address in the brain.

414
00:23:45,630 --> 00:23:47,070
In two thousand ten,

415
00:23:47,130 --> 00:23:51,430
Stephen Fleming and colleagues measured
introspective accuracy in a group of people,

416
00:23:51,490 --> 00:23:53,300
and scanned their brains.

417
00:23:53,650 --> 00:23:58,310
Those with better metacognition had more
grey matter in the anterior prefrontal

418
00:23:58,370 --> 00:24:01,010
cortex: the very front of the brain,

419
00:24:01,070 --> 00:24:04,140
a region that has expanded
enormously in humans.

420
00:24:04,810 --> 00:24:09,580
Four years later, the same group studied
patients with damage to that region.

421
00:24:09,950 --> 00:24:11,580
Their perception was intact.

422
00:24:11,890 --> 00:24:14,050
Their performance on the task was normal.

423
00:24:14,110 --> 00:24:17,660
But their ability to know when
they were wrong was impaired.

424
00:24:17,970 --> 00:24:20,820
And, curiously, only for perception:

425
00:24:21,230 --> 00:24:24,620
their metacognition about their
own memory was untouched.

426
00:24:24,910 --> 00:24:28,120
Even the meta level has
its own specialised parts.

427
00:24:28,470 --> 00:24:30,100
So which is it?

428
00:24:30,290 --> 00:24:34,620
A moment ago, confidence was readable
in the very circuit that decides.

429
00:24:35,130 --> 00:24:38,120
Now it lives somewhere
else. Both are true.

430
00:24:38,390 --> 00:24:43,340
The raw signal is computed where the decision
is made: how far the counter climbed.

431
00:24:43,630 --> 00:24:47,570
Reading that signal, and turning it
into a judgment you can use or report,

432
00:24:47,630 --> 00:24:50,700
takes other regions,
at the front of the brain.

433
00:24:50,910 --> 00:24:53,600
And that second step is
the one that can break.

434
00:24:53,950 --> 00:24:58,180
Knowing, and knowing that you know,
live in different places.

435
00:24:58,470 --> 00:25:00,310
And if they live in different places,

436
00:25:00,370 --> 00:25:03,560
then one of them can break
while the other keeps working.

437
00:25:04,430 --> 00:25:06,880
That is exactly what neurology shows.

438
00:25:07,250 --> 00:25:10,280
Two conditions, mirror
images of each other.

439
00:25:10,930 --> 00:25:11,930
Blindsight.

440
00:25:12,070 --> 00:25:17,120
Patients with damage to the visual cortex
are blind in part of their visual field.

441
00:25:17,270 --> 00:25:21,300
Show a light there, and they tell you,
honestly, that they see nothing.

442
00:25:21,650 --> 00:25:25,920
Ask them to guess where it was,
and they point to it, far above chance.

443
00:25:26,630 --> 00:25:30,940
Performance without metacognition.
The object level works.

444
00:25:31,190 --> 00:25:33,240
The meta level reports nothing.

445
00:25:33,470 --> 00:25:36,720
The first line is intact;
the second is blank.

446
00:25:37,210 --> 00:25:38,460
And the mirror image.

447
00:25:38,930 --> 00:25:43,090
Anosognosia, described by
Babinski in nineteen fourteen.

448
00:25:43,150 --> 00:25:48,510
Patients paralysed on one side after a
stroke who do not know they are paralysed.

449
00:25:48,570 --> 00:25:51,150
Ask them to raise their arm;
they say they have.

450
00:25:51,210 --> 00:25:53,000
They are not lying.

451
00:25:53,230 --> 00:25:57,060
The deficit is there, and the
knowledge of the deficit is missing.

452
00:25:57,370 --> 00:26:01,200
The second line says all is well;
the first says otherwise.

453
00:26:01,730 --> 00:26:04,340
Two functions. Two circuits.

454
00:26:04,630 --> 00:26:07,200
Each can fail while the
other keeps working.

455
00:26:07,470 --> 00:26:09,040
Remember these two patients.

456
00:26:09,230 --> 00:26:11,980
We will meet both of
them again, as machines.

457
00:26:12,490 --> 00:26:16,480
Let me gather all of this into one
picture before we leave the brain.

458
00:26:16,590 --> 00:26:19,650
In two thousand seventeen,
Stanislas Dehaene,

459
00:26:19,710 --> 00:26:23,880
Hakwan Lau and Sid Kouider
summarised it in three levels.

460
00:26:24,076 --> 00:26:27,180
C-zero: processing without awareness.

461
00:26:27,570 --> 00:26:30,560
Your brain corrects your posture,
and you never know.

462
00:26:30,756 --> 00:26:35,880
C-one: information made available
across the brain, what you can report.

463
00:26:36,118 --> 00:26:40,180
C-two: self-monitoring,
knowing you might be wrong.

464
00:26:40,268 --> 00:26:44,460
C-two is our meta level.
It is the second line.

465
00:26:44,830 --> 00:26:50,100
And their paper's title asked a question
close to ours: What is consciousness,

466
00:26:50,410 --> 00:26:52,220
and could machines have it?

467
00:26:52,510 --> 00:26:54,460
We will leave consciousness aside.

468
00:26:54,626 --> 00:26:58,340
Self-monitoring, C-two,
is something we can test.

469
00:26:58,990 --> 00:27:01,920
So, three things we now know how to do.

470
00:27:02,130 --> 00:27:06,620
Read confidence in behaviour:
the escape, the wait, the bet.

471
00:27:06,830 --> 00:27:10,800
Read it in a neuron. And measure
it apart from performance.

472
00:27:11,010 --> 00:27:13,780
We are about to do all three on a machine.

473
00:27:13,930 --> 00:27:16,960
And the machine has already
shown us its doubt once.

474
00:27:22,550 --> 00:27:26,480
This last act is about a question
you have probably asked yourself.

475
00:27:26,870 --> 00:27:29,940
When a chatbot answers you,
should you believe it?

476
00:27:30,290 --> 00:27:32,490
Let us go back to the
machine from the beginning,

477
00:27:32,550 --> 00:27:34,620
and look at what it is really doing.

478
00:27:34,710 --> 00:27:38,530
Because the doubt we saw in the first
minute has to come from somewhere,

479
00:27:38,590 --> 00:27:41,800
and the place it comes from
is surprisingly simple.

480
00:27:42,070 --> 00:27:46,400
In the first minute, I said this
program predicts the next word.

481
00:27:46,750 --> 00:27:50,780
That is not quite right, and the
difference is the whole story.

482
00:27:51,030 --> 00:27:52,640
It does not pick a word.

483
00:27:52,790 --> 00:27:57,960
It produces a probability for every word,
and piece of word, in its vocabulary.

484
00:27:58,210 --> 00:28:01,960
Over a hundred thousand of them,
for this model, all at once.

485
00:28:02,310 --> 00:28:04,680
Your phone does a tiny version of this.

486
00:28:04,790 --> 00:28:08,040
The three words above your
keyboard are the top of its list.

487
00:28:08,210 --> 00:28:12,480
Take a sentence like this one:
"The capital of Chile is".

488
00:28:12,770 --> 00:28:16,340
One bar towers over everything. Santiago.

489
00:28:17,170 --> 00:28:21,740
Now this one: "The capital of the
Martian colony Alpha-three is".

490
00:28:22,070 --> 00:28:23,760
And the bars spread out.

491
00:28:23,970 --> 00:28:26,760
Dozens of candidates,
none of them convincing.

492
00:28:26,950 --> 00:28:30,180
Look at the shape of that.
Peaked, then flat.

493
00:28:30,450 --> 00:28:32,160
That shape is the doubt.

494
00:28:32,590 --> 00:28:35,860
And now a small experiment
with a large consequence.

495
00:28:36,060 --> 00:28:38,960
What if you force it to
finish the sentence anyway?

496
00:28:39,350 --> 00:28:41,200
I did not expect the answer.

497
00:28:41,450 --> 00:28:45,200
It writes: a sprawling
metropolis known as New Eden.

498
00:28:45,350 --> 00:28:48,080
It invents a city. Confidently.

499
00:28:48,150 --> 00:28:52,140
The same model that, asked properly,
had told us it knew nothing.

500
00:28:52,690 --> 00:28:54,820
Look at the two halves of the screen.

501
00:28:55,090 --> 00:28:58,140
The words are sure. The bars are not.

502
00:28:58,310 --> 00:29:02,370
The doubt was there, in the shape,
and the sentence went ahead without it.

503
00:29:02,430 --> 00:29:05,930
The rule from act one is
right here, in a machine,

504
00:29:05,990 --> 00:29:10,120
in one screen: the verbal
report is not the measurement.

505
00:29:10,790 --> 00:29:14,000
That shape, peaked or flat, is a number.

506
00:29:14,270 --> 00:29:16,960
It is called the entropy
of the distribution.

507
00:29:17,150 --> 00:29:19,760
And nobody asked the model to compute it.

508
00:29:19,830 --> 00:29:23,620
It is a by-product of the main
thing the model was trained to do.

509
00:29:24,130 --> 00:29:25,240
Here is why.

510
00:29:25,470 --> 00:29:28,600
During training,
the model reads real text.

511
00:29:28,850 --> 00:29:30,750
At every word, it gets a score:

512
00:29:30,810 --> 00:29:35,520
how much probability did it give to
the word that actually came next?

513
00:29:35,630 --> 00:29:40,380
If it puts all its probability on the
wrong word, the penalty is enormous.

514
00:29:40,630 --> 00:29:44,920
So the training itself punishes
confidence in the wrong place.

515
00:29:45,070 --> 00:29:48,050
The cheapest strategy,
over trillions of words,

516
00:29:48,110 --> 00:29:51,010
is to be confident exactly
where the text is predictable

517
00:29:51,070 --> 00:29:53,620
and to spread out where it is not.

518
00:29:53,970 --> 00:29:59,340
So the monitoring signal is already there,
for free, in the shape of the output.

519
00:29:59,510 --> 00:30:02,480
Not a view from the inside: a signal.

520
00:30:02,810 --> 00:30:07,060
That was the question from act one,
and for machines, this is the answer.

521
00:30:07,290 --> 00:30:09,920
It also tells us where
the doubt comes from.

522
00:30:10,050 --> 00:30:12,260
And it opens two harder questions.

523
00:30:12,650 --> 00:30:14,160
Is the signal accurate?

524
00:30:14,410 --> 00:30:16,500
And is anyone listening to it?

525
00:30:16,870 --> 00:30:18,200
Accurate first.

526
00:30:18,610 --> 00:30:21,080
You met the test in act two: calibration.

527
00:30:21,590 --> 00:30:25,880
When it says seventy percent,
is it right seventy percent of the time?

528
00:30:26,270 --> 00:30:30,080
We can draw this for a machine
exactly as we drew it for a person.

529
00:30:30,290 --> 00:30:35,140
Confidence on one axis, how often the
answer is actually right on the other.

530
00:30:35,410 --> 00:30:37,620
Perfect calibration is the diagonal.

531
00:30:38,050 --> 00:30:42,850
A curve that sags below it means
overconfidence: the system says ninety,

532
00:30:42,910 --> 00:30:44,750
and is right sixty.

533
00:30:44,810 --> 00:30:46,334
In two thousand seventeen,

534
00:30:46,394 --> 00:30:51,280
Chuan Guo and colleagues drew that curve
for modern image-recognition networks.

535
00:30:51,790 --> 00:30:53,660
The result was uncomfortable.

536
00:30:54,270 --> 00:30:58,160
The networks of the nineteen-nineties
had been reasonably calibrated.

537
00:30:58,450 --> 00:31:03,880
The deep networks of twenty sixteen,
far more accurate, were badly overconfident.

538
00:31:04,110 --> 00:31:08,640
They had become better at being right,
and worse at knowing when they were wrong.

539
00:31:08,790 --> 00:31:11,660
Progress in one, regression in the other.

540
00:31:12,230 --> 00:31:16,360
And they found a fix that is almost
embarrassing in its simplicity.

541
00:31:16,690 --> 00:31:17,700
One number.

542
00:31:17,890 --> 00:31:22,060
A "temperature" that softens every
distribution by the same amount.

543
00:31:22,290 --> 00:31:25,630
Tune that single number on a
handful of questions set aside,

544
00:31:25,690 --> 00:31:28,600
and the curve snaps back to the diagonal.

545
00:31:29,090 --> 00:31:34,137
So, for this film, I drew that curve for
the open model from the first minute.

546
00:31:34,197 --> 00:31:36,900
Six hundred general-knowledge questions.

547
00:31:37,150 --> 00:31:41,030
For each one, the probability it gave
to the first word of its answer,

548
00:31:41,090 --> 00:31:43,380
against whether the answer was right.

549
00:31:43,850 --> 00:31:45,920
I expected to have to correct it.

550
00:31:46,170 --> 00:31:49,900
Here is the honest result:
there was almost nothing to fix.

551
00:31:50,170 --> 00:31:51,960
The curve sits on the diagonal.

552
00:31:52,210 --> 00:31:56,040
If anything, in the middle,
this model is slightly under-confident:

553
00:31:56,190 --> 00:31:57,890
it is right more often than it says.

554
00:31:57,950 --> 00:32:02,310
Do not generalise: other models,
after their final training,

555
00:32:02,370 --> 00:32:05,960
have been reported to drift the
other way, towards overconfidence.

556
00:32:06,270 --> 00:32:09,780
But this one, on these questions,
keeps its numbers honest.

557
00:32:11,450 --> 00:32:13,520
And yet: New Eden.

558
00:32:13,850 --> 00:32:18,260
Honest numbers on the next word,
and a city invented in the next sentence.

559
00:32:18,590 --> 00:32:22,360
Calibration at the word is not
truthfulness at the sentence.

560
00:32:22,550 --> 00:32:25,300
That gap is what this whole act is about.

561
00:32:25,690 --> 00:32:29,900
So the signal is there, and on
simple questions it is honest.

562
00:32:30,310 --> 00:32:33,990
The next question is the one
from act two: can it be read,

563
00:32:34,050 --> 00:32:38,720
from behaviour or from the inside,
the way we read the dolphin and the monkey?

564
00:32:39,010 --> 00:32:43,640
In twenty twenty-two, a team of
researchers asked exactly that.

565
00:32:43,830 --> 00:32:48,640
Their paper was titled Language
Models (Mostly) Know What They Know.

566
00:32:48,850 --> 00:32:53,290
They tried two things,
and each one has an echo in act two.

567
00:32:53,350 --> 00:32:56,120
First, they let the
model answer a question,

568
00:32:56,270 --> 00:32:59,960
then showed it its own answer
and asked: is this true?

569
00:33:00,170 --> 00:33:04,050
The model gives a probability;
they called it P-true.

570
00:33:04,110 --> 00:33:06,420
A second look, after the answer.

571
00:33:06,570 --> 00:33:09,950
That is the error signal
from act two, in a machine.

572
00:33:10,010 --> 00:33:12,780
And it was a fairly good
judge of its own answers.

573
00:33:13,210 --> 00:33:15,960
Second, the result that should stop you.

574
00:33:16,070 --> 00:33:20,280
Could they tell, before the model said
anything, whether it knew the answer?

575
00:33:20,470 --> 00:33:23,640
They did not ask it in words.
They opened it up.

576
00:33:24,050 --> 00:33:26,840
The next part is the most
technical of the film.

577
00:33:26,990 --> 00:33:29,960
Stay with me. It is also the heart of it.

578
00:33:30,290 --> 00:33:32,720
A language model is a stack of layers.

579
00:33:33,090 --> 00:33:37,300
At each layer, the input is
represented as a long list of numbers:

580
00:33:37,450 --> 00:33:39,080
an activation vector.

581
00:33:39,450 --> 00:33:44,630
The researchers added one small extra
output to the model: a single number,

582
00:33:44,690 --> 00:33:49,120
read from that internal state before
the model had written a single word.

583
00:33:49,430 --> 00:33:53,460
They trained it to predict one thing:
will the answer be right?

584
00:33:53,810 --> 00:33:58,060
They called it P-I-K,
the probability that "I know".

585
00:33:58,330 --> 00:34:01,560
That is Hart's feeling of
knowing, in a machine.

586
00:34:02,090 --> 00:34:03,280
It worked.

587
00:34:03,450 --> 00:34:07,660
The model could predict, in advance,
which questions it would get right.

588
00:34:07,820 --> 00:34:11,740
On the questions it was trained on,
the number was well calibrated.

589
00:34:11,910 --> 00:34:15,780
On entirely new kinds of problems,
like code or mathematics,

590
00:34:16,130 --> 00:34:20,950
it still separated what the model could
answer from what it could not: less well,

591
00:34:21,010 --> 00:34:22,460
but better than chance.

592
00:34:22,690 --> 00:34:25,280
And it got better as
the models grew larger.

593
00:34:25,550 --> 00:34:29,100
Later groups showed that something
even simpler is often enough:

594
00:34:29,170 --> 00:34:34,000
a straight line through the activations,
with the model itself left untouched.

595
00:34:34,150 --> 00:34:36,640
That simple reader is called a probe.

596
00:34:36,950 --> 00:34:41,410
The information "I know this" or
"I do not know this" was there,

597
00:34:41,470 --> 00:34:46,420
in the activations, readable with a ruler,
before the model had said anything.

598
00:34:46,830 --> 00:34:48,920
Stop and look at what that is.

599
00:34:49,070 --> 00:34:52,220
This is Kiani and Shadlen's
idea, in a machine.

600
00:34:52,550 --> 00:34:55,160
The confidence is not
in a separate module.

601
00:34:55,270 --> 00:34:58,470
It is readable in the same
circuit that produces the answer,

602
00:34:58,530 --> 00:35:00,740
before the answer comes out.

603
00:35:01,050 --> 00:35:04,180
The counter, again,
this time made of numbers.

604
00:35:04,790 --> 00:35:06,720
And think about where it came from.

605
00:35:06,930 --> 00:35:09,470
None of the trillions of
words this model was trained

606
00:35:09,530 --> 00:35:13,020
on carried a label saying
"this is a hard question".

607
00:35:13,210 --> 00:35:15,420
The signal was never taught directly.

608
00:35:15,610 --> 00:35:18,830
It emerged, from the pressure
we saw a moment ago:

609
00:35:18,890 --> 00:35:21,920
to be confident only where
the text is predictable.

610
00:35:22,390 --> 00:35:24,140
One honest footnote.

611
00:35:24,450 --> 00:35:27,570
The makers of this model did,
at the very end,

612
00:35:27,630 --> 00:35:30,860
train it to decline questions
it kept getting wrong.

613
00:35:31,130 --> 00:35:34,880
That is most likely where its
polite refusal comes from.

614
00:35:35,050 --> 00:35:38,150
But a lesson like that only
works if something inside

615
00:35:38,210 --> 00:35:41,260
already tells the two
kinds of question apart.

616
00:35:41,490 --> 00:35:45,210
And when we forced it to finish the
sentence, the refusal was gone,

617
00:35:45,270 --> 00:35:47,300
and New Eden appeared.

618
00:35:47,770 --> 00:35:50,430
That is the result from twenty twenty-two.

619
00:35:50,490 --> 00:35:53,340
Let me show you the same
thing, done by hand.

620
00:35:53,670 --> 00:35:55,960
The model is the one
from the first minute:

621
00:35:56,070 --> 00:35:58,820
an open model of eight billion parameters.

622
00:35:59,210 --> 00:36:02,320
The probe is the simplest
kind: a straight line.

623
00:36:02,770 --> 00:36:04,000
Here are some questions.

624
00:36:04,350 --> 00:36:07,470
For each one, before the
model generates its answer,

625
00:36:07,530 --> 00:36:10,700
the probe reads the activations
and lights up a gauge.

626
00:36:10,890 --> 00:36:14,460
A probe trained on other questions,
and tested on these.

627
00:36:14,630 --> 00:36:16,200
Then the model answers.

628
00:36:16,470 --> 00:36:17,540
Then we check.

629
00:36:17,910 --> 00:36:19,000
Watch the gauge.

630
00:36:19,410 --> 00:36:23,200
When it is low, the answer that
follows is more often wrong.

631
00:36:23,470 --> 00:36:25,740
When it is high, usually right.

632
00:36:26,090 --> 00:36:30,120
The model has not spoken yet.
The signal is already there.

633
00:36:30,470 --> 00:36:35,220
If you have used a chatbot, you have
watched its answer appear word by word.

634
00:36:35,370 --> 00:36:38,020
This gauge moves before the first one.

635
00:36:38,370 --> 00:36:43,180
One number sums it all up.
Pick a right answer and a wrong one.

636
00:36:43,390 --> 00:36:46,360
How often does the probe
trust the right one more?

637
00:36:46,790 --> 00:36:48,740
Point five is a coin toss.

638
00:36:49,110 --> 00:36:50,540
One is perfect.

639
00:36:51,010 --> 00:36:53,680
That is the sensitivity from act two:

640
00:36:53,950 --> 00:36:57,480
does the signal separate the right
answers from the wrong ones?

641
00:36:57,750 --> 00:37:00,510
The ladder on screen
climbs from point seven to

642
00:37:00,570 --> 00:37:03,820
point eight-four as we read
deeper into the model.

643
00:37:04,070 --> 00:37:08,730
To be fair, the shape of the output,
the bars from the start of this act,

644
00:37:08,790 --> 00:37:12,106
does about as well overall:
point eight-five.

645
00:37:12,170 --> 00:37:15,280
What the probe adds is
what the shape cannot see.

646
00:37:15,470 --> 00:37:17,390
Among the answers that looked certain,

647
00:37:17,450 --> 00:37:20,220
it still tells the right
ones from the wrong ones.

648
00:37:20,530 --> 00:37:25,320
So: a meta level. Or at least
its first half: monitoring.

649
00:37:25,650 --> 00:37:28,400
The signal exists, and we can read it.

650
00:37:28,590 --> 00:37:32,000
The second half of the diagram
is the arrow going down.

651
00:37:32,230 --> 00:37:33,210
Control.

652
00:37:33,270 --> 00:37:37,220
And that is where the word
"metacognition" starts to get slippery.

653
00:37:37,830 --> 00:37:40,960
This is where the research
literature becomes confusing,

654
00:37:41,070 --> 00:37:45,000
and I want to slow down,
because the confusion is instructive.

655
00:37:45,210 --> 00:37:48,370
Once models started showing
signs of self-monitoring,

656
00:37:48,430 --> 00:37:51,720
the word "metacognition"
was applied to everything.

657
00:37:51,930 --> 00:37:55,560
A model that says "I think".
A confidence score.

658
00:37:55,750 --> 00:37:59,520
A system that sends each word
to a specialised sub-network.

659
00:37:59,670 --> 00:38:02,580
A model that thinks
longer on hard problems.

660
00:38:02,750 --> 00:38:04,720
All of it, the same word.

661
00:38:05,250 --> 00:38:07,520
So let us impose some discipline.

662
00:38:07,790 --> 00:38:11,320
Three tests, taken straight
from acts one and two.

663
00:38:11,910 --> 00:38:15,740
One: the signal must be
about the model's own state,

664
00:38:15,850 --> 00:38:20,320
its activations or its output distribution,
not just about the input.

665
00:38:20,630 --> 00:38:23,410
A separate program that
guesses "this question looks

666
00:38:23,470 --> 00:38:27,350
hard" from the question
alone is not metacognition.

667
00:38:27,410 --> 00:38:29,100
It is not looking inside.

668
00:38:29,270 --> 00:38:31,780
It is not a second line about the first.

669
00:38:32,410 --> 00:38:37,740
Two: the signal must be used.
It must change what the system does.

670
00:38:38,130 --> 00:38:42,190
A confidence score that only a
researcher ever reads is a measurement,

671
00:38:42,250 --> 00:38:43,740
not a meta level.

672
00:38:44,010 --> 00:38:45,940
The dolphin pressed the paddle.

673
00:38:46,530 --> 00:38:50,300
Three: the mechanism must be identifiable:

674
00:38:50,490 --> 00:38:53,680
ideally something you could
switch off and see what changes.

675
00:38:53,870 --> 00:38:55,300
Like Fleming's patients.

676
00:38:55,950 --> 00:39:01,360
With those tests, here is one way to sort
the literature: seven distinct functions.

677
00:39:01,670 --> 00:39:05,360
Three of them are sensors:
they produce a monitoring signal.

678
00:39:05,550 --> 00:39:09,530
Four are regulators:
they consume it to control behaviour.

679
00:39:09,590 --> 00:39:13,400
Nelson and Narens' diagram,
once more. Let's walk through them.

680
00:39:13,930 --> 00:39:17,040
Sensor one, calibration. We have seen it.

681
00:39:17,150 --> 00:39:20,030
Does seventy percent mean seventy percent?

682
00:39:20,090 --> 00:39:25,320
The oldest, the best measured, and still
not solved for long free-form answers.

683
00:39:26,250 --> 00:39:28,800
Sensor two, out-of-domain detection.

684
00:39:29,250 --> 00:39:30,940
Am I on familiar ground?

685
00:39:31,250 --> 00:39:35,440
A family doctor knows when a case is
beyond her and refers to a specialist.

686
00:39:35,750 --> 00:39:38,960
For a language model,
the failure is the hallucination:

687
00:39:39,110 --> 00:39:42,020
a fluent, plausible, false answer.

688
00:39:42,210 --> 00:39:44,530
You may have seen it yourself:
ask for a reference,

689
00:39:44,590 --> 00:39:49,380
and it invents one that looks real.
New Eden, with a bibliography.

690
00:39:49,950 --> 00:39:54,720
One clever detector: ask the model
the same question five times.

691
00:39:54,930 --> 00:39:57,320
If it knows, the five answers agree.

692
00:39:57,510 --> 00:40:00,340
If it is inventing,
they contradict each other.

693
00:40:00,690 --> 00:40:03,240
Inconsistency as a symptom of ignorance.

694
00:40:03,830 --> 00:40:07,050
And a striking finding
from twenty twenty-five.

695
00:40:07,110 --> 00:40:08,700
Inside a language model,

696
00:40:08,760 --> 00:40:13,510
researchers found internal directions that
seem to mean "I recognise this name",

697
00:40:13,570 --> 00:40:15,290
and "I don't".

698
00:40:15,350 --> 00:40:20,200
Push the model towards "I don't", and it
refuses to answer about Michael Jordan.

699
00:40:20,350 --> 00:40:25,500
Push it towards "I do", and it invents
facts about an athlete who does not exist.

700
00:40:25,770 --> 00:40:29,520
The sense of "I know this"
is a thing you can point at.

701
00:40:29,710 --> 00:40:31,956
Test three, passed.

702
00:40:32,050 --> 00:40:37,040
Sensor three looks across time.
Has the world changed since I learned?

703
00:40:37,270 --> 00:40:41,490
The tools come from factory quality
control in the nineteen fifties:

704
00:40:41,550 --> 00:40:45,500
watch your own error rate,
sound the alarm when it drifts.

705
00:40:45,710 --> 00:40:47,494
Sounding the alarm is easy.

706
00:40:47,554 --> 00:40:51,140
Relearning without forgetting
everything else is the hard part.

707
00:40:51,330 --> 00:40:55,638
It is like moving house: you notice in
a second that the flat is different;

708
00:40:55,698 --> 00:40:58,560
relearning the light switches takes weeks.

709
00:40:59,350 --> 00:41:00,840
Three ways to notice.

710
00:41:01,350 --> 00:41:07,180
Now, four ways to act on what you noticed:
the regulators, the arrow going down.

711
00:41:07,430 --> 00:41:09,250
The first: routing.

712
00:41:09,350 --> 00:41:12,620
How should I handle this:
fast, or carefully?

713
00:41:12,890 --> 00:41:17,220
The simplest version is a cascade:
a small, cheap model answers;

714
00:41:17,430 --> 00:41:21,960
if its confidence is low, the question
is passed to a large, expensive one.

715
00:41:22,070 --> 00:41:24,600
The sensor feeds the regulator directly.

716
00:41:24,710 --> 00:41:27,060
It is the escape paddle, for a computer.

717
00:41:27,550 --> 00:41:28,550
A caution.

718
00:41:28,790 --> 00:41:31,170
Some large models also route:

719
00:41:31,230 --> 00:41:35,120
they send each word to a different
specialised part of the network.

720
00:41:35,270 --> 00:41:40,440
But they decide from the word itself,
not from any internal sense of difficulty.

721
00:41:40,710 --> 00:41:44,006
By our first test,
that is not metacognition.

722
00:41:44,070 --> 00:41:48,230
Other designs learn an internal signal
that says "stop computing here",

723
00:41:48,290 --> 00:41:50,780
and that one does cross the line.

724
00:41:51,470 --> 00:41:56,440
Regulator two: effort.
Not which path, but how much.

725
00:41:56,890 --> 00:41:58,070
Here is a reasoning model,

726
00:41:58,130 --> 00:42:02,030
of the kind released in twenty
twenty-four and twenty twenty-five.

727
00:42:02,090 --> 00:42:05,890
Three problems: a simple sum,
a puzzle about two trains,

728
00:42:05,950 --> 00:42:08,720
and a hard question in number theory.

729
00:42:08,850 --> 00:42:11,980
Watch the length of its draft
before the final answer.

730
00:42:12,430 --> 00:42:15,340
Short. Longer. Much longer.

731
00:42:15,750 --> 00:42:19,520
For the sum: three hundred and
fifty-nine tokens of reasoning.

732
00:42:19,750 --> 00:42:21,800
Tokens are pieces of words.

733
00:42:22,090 --> 00:42:24,370
For the trains: eighteen hundred.

734
00:42:24,430 --> 00:42:27,550
For the number-theory problem:
fifty-seven hundred,

735
00:42:27,610 --> 00:42:29,940
and there it hit the limit I had set.

736
00:42:30,090 --> 00:42:33,580
The model spends more computation
where the problem is harder.

737
00:42:33,690 --> 00:42:36,880
So do you. Does the model
sense the difficulty?

738
00:42:37,090 --> 00:42:40,120
Or does a hard problem
simply take more steps?

739
00:42:40,230 --> 00:42:42,140
That is still an open question.

740
00:42:42,530 --> 00:42:45,320
And this turned out to be a serious lever.

741
00:42:45,610 --> 00:42:46,850
In twenty twenty-four,

742
00:42:46,910 --> 00:42:51,440
researchers took problems a small model
could already solve some of the time.

743
00:42:51,550 --> 00:42:56,020
Given more time to think, it could
beat a model fourteen times larger.

744
00:42:56,150 --> 00:43:00,950
And the best results came from matching the
effort to how hard each question looked.

745
00:43:01,010 --> 00:43:06,620
The instinct that bigger is always better
turned out to be, at least partly, wrong.

746
00:43:07,290 --> 00:43:09,840
Regulator three: exploration.

747
00:43:10,150 --> 00:43:12,440
Where should I look for what I am missing?

748
00:43:12,730 --> 00:43:18,450
In artificial agents this became "curiosity":
go where your own predictions fail,

749
00:43:18,510 --> 00:43:20,900
because that is where there
is something to learn.

750
00:43:21,090 --> 00:43:25,310
An agent dropped into Minecraft gives
itself tasks just beyond what it

751
00:43:25,370 --> 00:43:30,340
can already do: a wooden pickaxe,
then a stone one, then an iron one.

752
00:43:30,430 --> 00:43:32,940
It teaches itself to craft its tools.

753
00:43:33,710 --> 00:43:38,300
Regulator four, the last one,
and the one that matters most for you.

754
00:43:38,430 --> 00:43:40,580
The only one pointed at the human.

755
00:43:40,890 --> 00:43:45,320
Honest hedging: phrasing the answer
so that it reflects the confidence.

756
00:43:45,810 --> 00:43:49,560
"I think." "I'm not certain."
"You should check this."

757
00:43:49,970 --> 00:43:52,160
It is the most visible of the seven.

758
00:43:52,430 --> 00:43:54,940
It is what a user perceives as humility.

759
00:43:55,120 --> 00:43:57,380
It is also the most misleading.

760
00:43:57,770 --> 00:43:59,690
And it deserves its own section,

761
00:43:59,750 --> 00:44:03,180
because it is where the promise
from the first minute gets paid.

762
00:44:03,670 --> 00:44:05,100
Back to the stockings.

763
00:44:05,390 --> 00:44:07,600
Humans tell more than they know:

764
00:44:07,890 --> 00:44:12,260
the narrator speaks with confidence
about a process it cannot see.

765
00:44:12,730 --> 00:44:16,400
Language models, it turns out,
have the opposite problem.

766
00:44:16,690 --> 00:44:18,660
They know more than they tell.

767
00:44:18,950 --> 00:44:20,670
In twenty twenty-three,

768
00:44:20,730 --> 00:44:23,430
Aviv Slobodkin and colleagues
gave models questions

769
00:44:23,490 --> 00:44:26,780
that could not be answered from
the text in front of them.

770
00:44:26,990 --> 00:44:30,360
The models answered anyway,
fluently, confidently.

771
00:44:30,870 --> 00:44:31,870
Hallucinations.

772
00:44:32,430 --> 00:44:34,060
Then they looked inside.

773
00:44:34,350 --> 00:44:38,660
And in the activations, as early
as the first word of the answer,

774
00:44:38,810 --> 00:44:42,290
the model had already encoded that
the question was unanswerable.

775
00:44:42,350 --> 00:44:45,220
It knew. It said the opposite.

776
00:44:46,170 --> 00:44:48,500
The model from the first
minute does it too.

777
00:44:48,670 --> 00:44:50,940
Asked plainly, it refuses.

778
00:44:51,290 --> 00:44:55,200
But ask for a short answer and a
confidence score, and things change.

779
00:44:55,430 --> 00:44:57,790
On thirty questions that have no answer,

780
00:44:57,850 --> 00:45:02,040
it invents one six times,
with a confidence of eighty or more.

781
00:45:02,530 --> 00:45:05,200
And now, the promise
from the first minute.

782
00:45:05,390 --> 00:45:07,830
We asked it, on the same
six hundred questions,

783
00:45:07,890 --> 00:45:11,710
how confident it was,
from zero to one hundred.

784
00:45:11,770 --> 00:45:14,590
It said one hundred on
four hundred of them.

785
00:45:14,650 --> 00:45:16,766
Its average was ninety-five.

786
00:45:16,830 --> 00:45:19,290
When it was right, it said ninety-seven.

787
00:45:19,350 --> 00:45:21,776
When it was wrong, ninety-one.

788
00:45:21,990 --> 00:45:24,590
It was right about seven times in ten.

789
00:45:24,650 --> 00:45:27,530
And on the four hundred answers
where it said one hundred,

790
00:45:27,590 --> 00:45:32,430
it was right three times out of four.
That is the human figure from act two.

791
00:45:32,490 --> 00:45:34,130
Its next-word numbers were honest.

792
00:45:34,190 --> 00:45:38,840
The numbers it says out loud are
overconfident, the way ours are.

793
00:45:39,190 --> 00:45:43,280
How well do those numbers separate the
right answers from the wrong ones?

794
00:45:43,690 --> 00:45:48,030
Barely: point five-nine,
where a coin toss is point five.

795
00:45:48,090 --> 00:45:50,630
The probe, reading the
inside of the same model,

796
00:45:50,690 --> 00:45:53,198
on the same questions
and the same answers:

797
00:45:53,258 --> 00:45:56,340
point eight-one,
about what it scored before.

798
00:45:57,090 --> 00:46:01,210
Now look only at the four hundred
answers where it said one hundred.

799
00:46:01,270 --> 00:46:06,720
There, its words carry no information at all:
every one of them says the same thing.

800
00:46:06,870 --> 00:46:10,810
And the probe still tells
right from wrong: point eight.

801
00:46:10,870 --> 00:46:13,420
Put the three readings side by side.

802
00:46:13,630 --> 00:46:16,680
Its words: barely better than a coin toss.

803
00:46:16,990 --> 00:46:20,740
The shape of its output:
point eight-five, as we saw.

804
00:46:20,830 --> 00:46:25,460
The inside: about as good, and still
working where the output looks sure.

805
00:46:25,910 --> 00:46:27,040
Four questions.

806
00:46:27,230 --> 00:46:30,730
For all four, the model says one hundred.

807
00:46:30,790 --> 00:46:33,980
Raging Bull: the probe
reads high; correct.

808
00:46:34,350 --> 00:46:37,950
A candy-bar slogan:
the model says Coca-Cola,

809
00:46:38,010 --> 00:46:41,140
the probe reads low;
the answer was Snickers.

810
00:46:41,610 --> 00:46:44,820
Mull, the Scottish island: high; correct.

811
00:46:45,090 --> 00:46:47,300
A footballer with a biblical surname:

812
00:46:47,590 --> 00:46:51,410
the model says Geoff Hurst,
the probe reads low; wrong.

813
00:46:51,470 --> 00:46:55,860
The same word for all four.
The probe tells them apart.

814
00:46:57,410 --> 00:46:59,360
The words say almost nothing.

815
00:46:59,610 --> 00:47:01,860
The activations say a great deal.

816
00:47:01,970 --> 00:47:04,500
And the probe is the one
that tracks the truth.

817
00:47:04,710 --> 00:47:08,220
We opened the machine,
we read its doubt before it spoke,

818
00:47:08,310 --> 00:47:10,200
and we caught it saying the opposite.

819
00:47:10,810 --> 00:47:13,560
Why the gap? Part of it is us.

820
00:47:13,910 --> 00:47:16,820
Modern models are refined
with human feedback:

821
00:47:16,990 --> 00:47:19,980
people compare answers and
pick the one they prefer.

822
00:47:20,170 --> 00:47:23,560
And people prefer confident
answers. We all do.

823
00:47:23,750 --> 00:47:27,240
Trained on that preference,
a model learns to sound sure.

824
00:47:27,410 --> 00:47:29,720
The monitoring signal is still inside.

825
00:47:29,910 --> 00:47:33,220
What got weaker is the step
that puts it into words.

826
00:47:33,670 --> 00:47:38,330
Which brings us to the trap,
and back to the two patients from act two.

827
00:47:38,390 --> 00:47:42,940
Two models can look identical from
outside and be opposite inside.

828
00:47:43,350 --> 00:47:49,120
The first always speaks with assurance,
yet inside, a signal tracks its errors.

829
00:47:49,270 --> 00:47:54,220
Like the blindsight patient, it has the
information and does not report it.

830
00:47:54,450 --> 00:48:01,160
The second says "I'm not sure" on every answer,
by reflex, with no signal behind it.

831
00:48:01,270 --> 00:48:06,320
Like the anosognosia patient, its words
are cut off from what is going on inside.

832
00:48:06,510 --> 00:48:10,560
Only here, the words say doubt
instead of "all is well".

833
00:48:11,090 --> 00:48:14,020
Call the second case performative hedging.

834
00:48:14,110 --> 00:48:15,470
And nothing rules it out.

835
00:48:15,530 --> 00:48:19,050
A model can learn to sprinkle
"I think" everywhere,

836
00:48:19,110 --> 00:48:22,420
and pass a test of humility,
with nothing underneath.

837
00:48:22,910 --> 00:48:26,960
Which is why the rule from act
one applies here with full force.

838
00:48:27,330 --> 00:48:30,100
The verbal report is not the measurement.

839
00:48:30,410 --> 00:48:34,560
To know whether a model knows
what it knows, you do not ask it.

840
00:48:34,630 --> 00:48:40,820
You open it, and you measure, as Fleming
measured his patients, as Kiani read LIP.

841
00:48:41,490 --> 00:48:44,690
And there is an irony here,
and it is not a small one.

842
00:48:44,750 --> 00:48:49,850
The best measurements, the numbers under
the words and the activations behind them,

843
00:48:49,910 --> 00:48:52,380
are the ones most users never see.

844
00:48:52,470 --> 00:48:56,120
Most of the world talks to these
models through a closed interface.

845
00:48:56,310 --> 00:49:00,160
The laboratory reads the numbers.
The product reads the words.

846
00:49:00,690 --> 00:49:03,600
So what can you do, with only the words?

847
00:49:03,950 --> 00:49:04,950
Two things.

848
00:49:05,030 --> 00:49:08,860
Treat a confident tone as a
report, not a measurement.

849
00:49:09,090 --> 00:49:13,610
And when it matters, ask the same
question again, in a fresh conversation.

850
00:49:13,670 --> 00:49:19,440
When it knows, the answers agree.
When it is inventing, they drift apart.

851
00:49:19,990 --> 00:49:25,220
So let me step back, and ask the only
question that matters for the next few years.

852
00:49:25,830 --> 00:49:28,140
What should we want from these systems?

853
00:49:28,610 --> 00:49:30,720
Not a model that is always right.

854
00:49:30,890 --> 00:49:34,340
That does not exist,
for machines or for us.

855
00:49:34,570 --> 00:49:36,360
Socrates already knew that.

856
00:49:36,610 --> 00:49:40,030
What we should want is a model
that knows when it might be wrong,

857
00:49:40,090 --> 00:49:44,700
and says so, and acts on it.
The dolphin's third paddle.

858
00:49:45,030 --> 00:49:49,380
Seen this way, a hallucination is
often not a failure of knowledge.

859
00:49:49,570 --> 00:49:53,010
It is a failure of the
meta level, of C-two.

860
00:49:53,070 --> 00:49:55,800
The signal was there. Nothing listened.

861
00:49:56,510 --> 00:49:57,830
And there is a tempting belief

862
00:49:57,890 --> 00:50:01,320
that all of this will simply
arrive with bigger models.

863
00:50:01,750 --> 00:50:05,660
For most of the seven,
the evidence so far does not show it.

864
00:50:05,810 --> 00:50:09,500
Calibration does improve with
scale, on short answers.

865
00:50:09,870 --> 00:50:12,840
Drift is the exception, for a dull reason:

866
00:50:13,010 --> 00:50:17,100
if the new world was in the training data,
there is nothing left to notice.

867
00:50:17,410 --> 00:50:22,320
The rest does not come by itself:
knowing when you are on unfamiliar ground,

868
00:50:22,380 --> 00:50:26,180
routing, effort,
exploration, honest hedging.

869
00:50:26,370 --> 00:50:28,440
Larger models are more accurate.

870
00:50:28,690 --> 00:50:33,080
They do not, by themselves, tell us
more honestly where their limits are.

871
00:50:33,230 --> 00:50:37,540
These functions have to be built,
measured, and verified, separately.

872
00:50:37,930 --> 00:50:41,400
Open questions remain,
and I want to be honest about them.

873
00:50:41,650 --> 00:50:45,060
Do these functions emerge at
scales we have not reached?

874
00:50:45,270 --> 00:50:48,440
How do the seven interact
inside one system?

875
00:50:48,690 --> 00:50:51,990
And how do we close the gap
between what the probes can read

876
00:50:52,050 --> 00:50:54,720
and what the products actually expose?

877
00:50:55,190 --> 00:50:59,520
Socrates claimed one thing:
to know what he did not know.

878
00:51:00,010 --> 00:51:02,230
Two and a half thousand years later,

879
00:51:02,290 --> 00:51:05,780
a machine built to predict
words has learned some of that.

880
00:51:05,910 --> 00:51:07,660
Partly. Silently.

881
00:51:07,990 --> 00:51:11,940
Mostly without being asked.
The knowledge is in its circuits.

882
00:51:12,330 --> 00:51:15,400
What it has not learned
is to tell us, reliably.

883
00:51:15,488 --> 00:51:20,820
Comte was right about one thing.
Nobody can watch themselves think.

884
00:51:20,990 --> 00:51:22,900
Not us, not the machine.

885
00:51:23,750 --> 00:51:25,580
But a signal can be measured.

886
00:51:25,830 --> 00:51:27,520
In a dolphin's hesitation.

887
00:51:27,790 --> 00:51:29,360
In a monkey's neurons.

888
00:51:29,670 --> 00:51:32,450
In a straight line through
the activations of a network.

889
00:51:32,510 --> 00:51:34,440
And in you.

890
00:51:34,670 --> 00:51:38,760
The next time a name is on the tip
of your tongue, notice that feeling.

891
00:51:39,070 --> 00:51:41,640
That is the second line, at work.

892
00:51:42,130 --> 00:51:44,820
Knowing what you know
is not only a feeling.

893
00:51:45,010 --> 00:51:48,280
It is a function.
And functions can be measured.

894
00:51:48,710 --> 00:51:49,820
Thanks for watching.

895
00:51:50,170 --> 00:51:53,440
The written notes, with every source,
are in the description.

896
00:51:53,810 --> 00:51:57,900
If you see a mistake, tell me.
That is rather the point of the film.
